  "query_preview": "/Through Saturday/...\nIssued at 1227 AM MDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nAn upper level low continues to spin over southern Utah tonight.\nComposite radar imagery across the state shows a few batches of\nprecipitation, mainly centered over southern and eastern Colorado\ntonight. There's also been some decent snow accumulation across\nportions of Park county over the last several hours, with some\ntravel impacts noted across a few of the mountain passes in that\narea. Some snow should linger through the night, though \nadditional accumulation there should be fairly light.\n\nThe upper low should continue its slow journey northeastward on\nSunday, leading to one last day of widespread rain and snow across\nthe region. While the track of this has remained consistent over\nthe last couple of days, there's still a few parts of the forecast\nthat remain rather uncertain. \n\nFirst, it looks likely that temperatures will be warmer across \nthe metro, as deeper stratus has failed to develop tonight... and \nmost guidance actually clears out the I-25 corridor as a Denver \ncyclone develops in the morning. It wouldn't be surprising to see\na few 60-65F temperatures during the afternoon hours before deeper\ncloud cover redevelops. Second, there is considerable spread with\nprecipitation totals Sunday night into Monday. A strong vort max\nis expected to rotate around the base of the trough, pivoting from\nnorthwestern New Mexico into northeastern Colorado by Monday\nmorning. A weak surface low should develop in response to this,\nbut models place the center of this surface low as far west as\nGreeley (HRRR) to as far east as Burlington by 09z Monday. Model\nconsistency in this period is nowhere to be found. The wholesale\nmean solution would be for scattered rain/snow showers developing\nover the northern mountains during the afternoon, with a more\norganized precipitation shield developing north/northwest of the\nsurface low where stronger synoptic scale lift would combine with\ndeepening upslope flow. This likely ends up somewhere along our\nnorthern tier of counties, with at least a chance (30-50%) of \nsome half inch rainfall totals near the CO/WY border. \n\nAny precipitation left in the forecast area should quickly move\nout of the area during the morning hours Monday, with clearing\nskies and near normal temperatures expected. \n\nThe rest of the week looks rather quiet, with warmer and dry\nweather likely under broad southwesterly flow aloft. High\ntemperatures should end up in the 60s to low 70s across the plains\nmost days. Some moisture could return as early as Friday or\nSaturday as a weak shortwave moves through the region, though the\nflow aloft would again favor the terrain to the west/southwest of\nour CWA. Though details remain hazy, there are signals that the\nstorm track would remain fairly active from next weekend into\nthe first couple days of November.",
